#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bea8139-82a8-5c0a-9d3b-f3a47356773e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Expanded Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8b91811-afee-5123-9211-ac4edb79d649" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284252} {:answer/id #uuid "306d7bca-0245-5294-8828-101173ac40fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8b91811-afee-5123-9211-ac4edb79d649" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284252} :answer-field/id #uuid "5485474f-70ab-53a0-af9c-b79f4d892b6e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284251} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e74dfbf4-2770-58e7-9e26-cd17ad59f558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e74dfbf4-2770-58e7-9e26-cd17ad59f558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284255} :answer-field/id #uuid "8de8628f-853d-5764-b216-91572f695454" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284254} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "259635c7-6829-53af-a55c-da5bf265970d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "259635c7-6829-53af-a55c-da5bf265970d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284257} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ab9e1cf-5742-5c01-bd8e-12c277079b44" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "065744d0-6e0f-5671-bdb3-64fff73f5efa" :question/math-academy-id "q-271012" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}>0.$\n\n$x\\in $\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)⋃\\,($\n{{field-3}}$,\\,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+2).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe only solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=0.$ This root divides the number line into $2$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,0),\\,(0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| ${x}^{2}+2$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,0),$ and $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bea8139-82a8-5c0a-9d3b-f3a47356773e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Expanded Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fc5c2cb-9b4f-5aa8-a45c-67b627e0bc8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fc5c2cb-9b4f-5aa8-a45c-67b627e0bc8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284260} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b32a1a7-827f-5939-b151-3bb361521f67" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284259} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff2d3f0f-f7ed-576e-a48a-4bde06403a37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff2d3f0f-f7ed-576e-a48a-4bde06403a37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284262} :answer-field/id #uuid "0eac0100-633f-5797-bb7f-e42381336142" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284261} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e010184c-a8cd-596c-9b8e-a1bebf5ad505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e010184c-a8cd-596c-9b8e-a1bebf5ad505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284264} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e22d853-4f42-5c92-b6d1-478836a41a22" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284263} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e94c5def-9bcf-5591-8bc2-94272dacbf9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e94c5def-9bcf-5591-8bc2-94272dacbf9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284266} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc9e4fd1-caff-526a-956b-d1fdaeeb6d48" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c70bbcce-3318-5de9-9a45-73b7c5f6af37" :question/math-academy-id "q-271013" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{4}+10{x}^{3}-39{x}^{2}\\ge 0.$\n\n$x\\in $\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]⋃\\,{$\n{{field-3}}$}⋃\\,[$\n{{field-4}}$,$\n$\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-39{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+10x-39) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+13x-3x-39) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x(x+13)-3(x+13)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-3)(x+13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}(x-3)(x+13).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-13,$ $x=0,$ and $x=3.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-13),\\,(-13,0),\\,(0,3),\\,(3,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-13)$ | $(-13,0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+13$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-13),$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-13]\\cup {0}\\cup [3,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c880562-074d-593c-b7dc-80bb39499fb1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Polynomial Inequality when the Right-Hand Side is Nonzero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96340500-231e-5b8c-8284-b437c15bb31b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ [- 2,-1]∪[0, 4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131667} {:answer/id #uuid "be987d29-cc3f-5123-b666-86bd1604182c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: x ∈ (- ∞, 2]]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131668} {:answer/id #uuid "328eed7b-5822-5b00-8b0c-89e034a0d1e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: x ∈ (- ∞,-2]∪{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131669} {:answer/id #uuid "41e7f4ab-1195-5773-be57-fded9544f19f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: x ∈ [- 2, 0]∪[4, ∞)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131670} {:answer/id #uuid "5b6cb8d4-b6a7-5cbe-a3a6-a014ca5d2f93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: x ∈ {-2}∪[2, ∞)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be987d29-cc3f-5123-b666-86bd1604182c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: x ∈ (- ∞, 2]]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131668} :answer-field/id #uuid "8acbf6fc-5570-52ba-bcb5-0ddb757d030f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fadcf989-4bd6-56a5-ba1d-b0b439417b14" :question/math-academy-id "q-40290" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{3}-4x\\le 8-2{x}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-4x & \\le 8-2{x}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{3}-4x)-(8-2{x}^{2}) & \\le 0 \\\\ ({x}^{3}-4x)+(2{x}^{2}-8) & \\le 0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-4)+2({x}^{2}-4) & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+2)({x}^{2}-4) & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-2)(x+2) & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}(x-2) & \\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)^{2}(x-2).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$ These roots divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,2),\\,(2,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $(x+2)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,2].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e675f1c-9093-5f6e-a3b3-112d0bc8e516" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Higher-Order Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e52a9b25-88c7-5d4f-93a0-cc3f9be9d79a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284269} {:answer/id #uuid "8ed82a17-01c6-5571-8f16-0a483e78660e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-2,0)\\cup (2,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284270} {:answer/id #uuid "0312f049-c4c8-5a33-905d-e9e3ff4f80db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2)\\cup (6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284271} {:answer/id #uuid "696884b8-e9c5-5364-926e-f39d936c88d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284272} {:answer/id #uuid "9fed6441-d9ec-571b-9141-fabf73434047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0312f049-c4c8-5a33-905d-e9e3ff4f80db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2)\\cup (6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284271} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d1ba5d8-ed63-55a7-a29f-ee8ae832642d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b186bb96-7c5c-5acb-97ae-614b84168cc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-88495" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $x(x+2)(x-2)(x-6)>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=x(x+2)(x-2)(x-6).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ $x=2$ and $x=6.$ These roots divide the number line into $5$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(2,6),\\,(6,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,6)$ | $(6,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2),$ $(0,2)$ and $(6,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2)\\cup (6,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e675f1c-9093-5f6e-a3b3-112d0bc8e516" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Higher-Order Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41613a0e-3905-5bdf-bef7-150ab649c84f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284276} {:answer/id #uuid "331d0b53-c13d-5783-b8de-9e6bd5f4e415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-1,1)\\cup (1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284277} {:answer/id #uuid "0a3fac6e-911b-53ea-acf2-df41c92e181a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284278} {:answer/id #uuid "844f5297-fd58-5605-9dfb-731bcad92d80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284279} {:answer/id #uuid "42e5e501-b0f6-5636-9e9d-5d2fb81bd49a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-3,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "844f5297-fd58-5605-9dfb-731bcad92d80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284279} :answer-field/id #uuid "baf7d3d8-a659-5138-80d7-61919092bb24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a31f2e6-b214-5110-ba67-9c33b185f87d" :question/math-academy-id "q-90487" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $(1-x)^{2}(x-3)(x+1)>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-x)^{2}(x-3)(x+1).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-1,$ $x=1$  and $x=3.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-1),\\,(-1,1),\\,(1,3),\\,(3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,1)$ | $(1,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $(1-x)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$"}]}
